															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lt-flops" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735419); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1735419&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200911" alt="Lt Flops" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1735419)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lt-flops" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735419); return false;" >Lt Flops</a></span> <span class="odate time_1548879050 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Jan 2019 20:10</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4135976">
									

<p>I want to like this, I do. The conceit of an ancient deity classified as a modern-day anomalous entity is interesting to me. But I have numerous gripes with the execution that rob me of how much I would enjoy this. I think its reliance on other articles to tell its story is too prominent: I came here to read about and understand this anomaly, not to gain a better understanding of the established properties of other articles by proxy. There are also aspects of the story here that strain my suspension of disbelief, and I don't think there is quite enough here to separate itself from other mythological demigods-turned-SCPs out there.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">feedback/rant</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'll start off by explaining what I do like. I like the portrayal of the O5's employment of the reality bender. It makes sense in the contest of the article and the Foundation in general: reality benders are far too important to piss off, so having a placated reality bender on your staff is an exceptional boon.</p>
<p>After mulling this over, though, I actually think it's the execution of cliches here that doesn't work:</p>
<ul>
<li>The article doesn't make me feel that this entity is a spooky murder monster. It tells me that it is, and compares it to other articles in an attempt to display that, without going into the properties that make it so. When an SCP relies so heavily on other articles as a means of comparison, it does neither its own article <em>nor</em> any crosslinked articles any justice. It's essentially saying &quot;my entity is more powerful, look at what it can do to <em>these</em> powerful SCPs!&quot;. And that doesn't appeal to me.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>While it makes sense that the O5s would want to employ a powerful being, how they came to trust it doesn't. This actually ties in with my next gripe as well:</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The portal it can create. Why is the portal necessary to tell this story? If the entity can portal out of the site at any time, then why does it bother staying contained? I can't think of any reason why it would continue to be contained, other than because it's a Foundation employee that happens to know O5-3.</li>
</ul>
<p>These aren't issues unique to this article. I've seen issues plaguing cliche justification throughout the contest. The point of the contest is to recontextualize old cliches in a manner that gives them new life. They're cliches for a reason: they're overused concepts that do little to make a story original. You <em>can</em> use a cliche as a means of telling a better story, but you have to do extra story work to make your use of the concept unique and interesting in its own way. And while I don't think you were able to execute your concept that well here, I would love to see a version of this written after the contest that doesn't rely on cliches.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

