<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>to answer your first question, I don't think it's relying too much on crosslinks to other articles to tell it's story. I made this purely in mind as a Thaumiel that can contain Sapient Keters, and to do that without trying to kill them. Using fear as a way of keeping them in place was the perfect route. You are correct in your assumptions that this is nothing more than an employee for the Foundation. He literally said he wanted a job that would allow him to use his powers as a god, and being in the Foundation would do so. He's only classified as an SCP because the Council wants to and considering how many names and identities he's tsken over the centuries, I don't think he would mind being called anything else.</li>
<li>True. They don't really trust it, as it's only on the approval of O5-3 and their contract, but they really can't pass up an opportunity for an answer that takes care of their Keters that break out of containment once a week. Which also brings me to my next answer:</li>
<li>Firstly, the portals exist as a gateway of sorts into its dimension. This is answered in the addenda which clearly states that it is only allowed access to the site it is kept in. Gods cannot break their own laws and rules once they're set in stone. I'm sure that the Foundation is aware of this fact and uses it the way one would have a contract with a company.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>All in all, I do plan on revisiting this after the contest is over. I also plan on making revisions to this since the only reason why I posted it was because I was almost out of time to post.</p>

