<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">I&nbsp;had&nbsp;a&nbsp;comment&nbsp;planned&nbsp;out&nbsp;until&nbsp;the&nbsp;internet&nbsp;crashed.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">blehh...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>What I'll specifically point to is the issue Floppy points out is that there's not enough here to convince me the O5s would actually put this guy into service based off one O5's account and the scroll contract Deimos devised. Though I absolutely love Nine's reaction and outbursts.</p>
<p>The characterizations are better than him actually containing outside SCPs in the table I'd say. If and when you rewrite it, given how much space you're really allocated for a single SCP article, I'd focus on multiple iterations of different O5s meeting him through multiple wars.</p>
<p>This does a couple of things:</p>
<ul>
<li>You can reasonably go as far back as WW1 or hell even as early as the Napoleonic War if you want to expand on Deimos via journals or even first-hand accounts (if we're going off the premise that O5s are immortal, eldritch beings).</li>
<li>Gives variety and subtext to the entity. You see him grow throughout these eras. Perform amazing feats sure, but always show some character growth interspersed.</li>
<li>Much like 4450 which was namedropped, you can expand on different journal entry formats, character interactions, and POVs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because the character itself is strong. I think that part above all is worth salvaging upon rewrite. Now, I think you tried to do it through the table format, but in reality, I don't think that was the strongest performance of who he was. And I think its a case of readers instinctively drawn to the fact your using really big names which draws the eye away from the main entity's pathos itself.</p>
<p>If you want to keep the table list, I'd recommend applying that with very simple, Series I creatures with more compact descriptions. Ones where you can focus on the entity's main avenue of inflicting terror after two parties are already in conflict or &quot;in the midst of battle.&quot;</p>
<p>Elsewise, you could probably get away with having the O5's decision to even allow it near big-bad-keters and world enders like 2669 as a &quot;PENDING&quot; option, refocusing purely on the logs and Deimos' growth throughout eras.</p>
<p>Or even a single conflict. This entire thing could've been scoped to WW2 honestly with multiple O5s.</p>
<p>These are all just suggestions I had to problems I saw in the final piece. I wish I could've contacted you earlier but my hands were really tied this week.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>As it stands it's a novote atm though will change to upvote if the piece receives revisions. As it stands, I loved the logs man.</p>

